Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'coBRIM vemurafenib cobimetinib overall survival progression-free survival melanoma', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 2685 tokens): High-level context
- Current guidelines: NCCN lists combination immunotherapy as the preferred first-line option for unresectable/metastatic melanoma over BRAF+MEK inhibitor (BRAFi/MEKi) therapy.
- Evidence base spans cost/outcomes analyses, long-term targeted therapy outcomes, indirect comparisons among BRAFi/MEKi regimens and versus combination immunotherapy, and prognostic/response modifiers (LDH, number of metastatic sites, depth of response, early adverse events, concomitant medications).

Key studies and findings

1) Cost per outcome of nivolumab + relatlimab vs BRAFi + MEKi (Hanna et al., JMCP, 2025; DOI: 10.18553/jmcp.2025.25015)
- Objective: Evaluate cost per outcome for first-line nivolumab + relatlimab versus BRAF+MEK inhibitor combinations in unresectable/metastatic melanoma, in the context of NCCN preference for combination immunotherapy.
- Results not provided in abstract excerpt.

2) coBRIM 5-year follow-up: cobimetinib + vemurafenib vs vemurafenib (Ascierto et al., Clin Cancer Res, 2021; DOI: 10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-21-0809)
- Design: Randomized phase III (NCT01689519) in previously untreated BRAFV600 mutation–positive advanced melanoma.
- Finding: Addition of cobimetinib to vemurafenib improved progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS) versus vemurafenib; extended follow-up reports long-term outcomes (≥5 years since last randomization).

3) Indirect comparison in elevated LDH subgroup across pivotal trials (Glutsch et al., Acta Derm Venereol, 2020; DOI: 10.2340/00015555-3526)
- Method: Indirect comparison (Bucher method) of coBRIM, COMBI-v, and COLUMBUS Part 1 in patients with elevated baseline lactate dehydrogenase (LDH).
- Finding: Non-significant risk reduction for progression with vemurafenib + cobimetinib versus dabrafenib + trametinib and encorafenib + binimetinib in elevated LDH subgroup; may inform therapy choice in this poor-prognosis population.

4) Review of efficacy, safety, tolerability of approved BRAFi/MEKi regimens (Hamid et al., Cancers, 2019; DOI: 10.3390/cancers11111642)
- Context: No head-to-head trials among BRAFi/MEKi combinations.
- Content: Synthesizes comparative efficacy, safety, and tolerability data across approved combinations.

5) Network meta-analysis of three BRAFi + MEKi combinations (Consoli et al., Clin Transl Oncol, 2020; DOI: 10.1007/s12094-019-02207-7)
- Objective: Indirectly compare dabrafenib + trametinib, vemurafenib + cobimetinib, and encorafenib + binimetinib for OS, PFS, ORR, and safety.
- Note: No direct head-to-head trials; provides relative efficacy/safety estimates across regimens.

6) Validation of prognostic groups (LDH + metastatic organ sites) for vemurafenib + cobimetinib (Sorich et al., Curr Med Res Opin, 2020; DOI: 10.1097/CMR.0000000000000634)
- Cohort: 809 patients (240 vemu+cobi; 569 vemu) from coBRIM and BRIM-3.
- Findings for vemu+cobi:
  - Prognostic groups significantly separated OS (P < 0.001; c-statistic = 0.72) and PFS (P < 0.001; c-statistic = 0.65).
  - 2-year PFS ranged 3% (LDH ≥2x ULN) to 50% (normal LDH and ≤3 metastatic sites).
  - 2-year OS ranged 7% to 71% across groups.
- Similar significant separation in vemurafenib monotherapy cohort.
- Conclusion: LDH and number of metastatic organ sites are robust, transportable prognostic markers across targeted regimens.

7) Depth of response (DoR) and outcomes with cobimetinib ± vemurafenib (Lewis et al., Br J Cancer, 2019; DOI: 10.1038/s41416-019-0546-y)
- Pooled analysis: BRIM-2, BRIM-3, BRIM-7, coBRIM.
- Finding: Greater depth of tumor response is prognostic for improved survival, supporting DoR as a meaningful intermediate endpoint.

8) Acid-reducing agents (ARAs), vemurafenib dose, and outcomes (Lewis et al., Eur J Cancer, 2019; DOI: 10.1016/j.ejca.2019.05.002)
- Retrospective analysis of the impact of concomitant ARAs and vemurafenib dosing on efficacy in BRAF-mutant melanoma.
- Aim: Clarify potential drug–drug interactions and dose–effect relationships; specific outcome data n
