Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'SECOMBIT phase II 4-year survival Nature Communications 2024 metastatic BRAF V600 melanoma', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"38167503","title":"Sequential immunotherapy and targeted therapy for metastatic BRAF V600 mutated melanoma: 4-year survival and biomarkers evaluation from the phase II SECOMBIT trial.","authors":"Ascierto PA; Casula M; Bulgarelli J; Pisano M; Piccinini C; Piccin L; Cossu A; Mandalà M; Ferrucci PF; Guidoboni M; Rutkowski P; Ferraresi V; Arance A; Guida M; Maiello E; Gogas H; Richtig E; Fierro MT; Lebbe C; Helgadottir H; Queirolo P; Spagnolo F; Tucci M; Del Vecchio M; Cao MG; Minisini AM; De Placido S; Sanmamed MF; Mallardo D; Paone M; Vitale MG; Melero I; Grimaldi AM; Giannarelli D; Dummer R; Sileni VC; Palmieri G","abstract":"No prospective data were available prior to 2021 to inform selection between combination BRAF and MEK inhibition versus dual blockade of programmed cell death protein-1 (PD-1) and cytotoxic T lymphocyte antigen-4 (CTLA-4) as first-line treatment options for BRAFV600-mutant melanoma. SECOMBIT (NCT02631447) was a randomized, three-arm, noncomparative phase II trial in which patients were randomized to one of two sequences with immunotherapy or targeted therapy first, with a third arm in which an 8-week induction course of targeted therapy followed by a planned switch to immunotherapy was the first treatment. BRAF/MEK inhibitors were encorafenib plus binimetinib and checkpoint inhibitors ipilimumab plus nivolumab. Primary outcome of overall survival was previously reported, demonstrating improved survival with immunotherapy administered until progression and followed by BRAF/MEK inhibition. Here we report 4-year survival outcomes, confirming long-term benefit with first-line immunotherapy. We also describe preliminary results of predefined biomarkers analyses that identify a trend toward improved 4-year overall survival and total progression-free survival in patients with loss-of-function mutations affecting JAK or low baseline levels of serum interferon gamma (IFNy). These long-term survival outcomes confirm immunotherapy as the preferred first-line treatment approach for most patients with BRAFV600-mutant metastatic melanoma, and the biomarker analyses are hypothesis-generating for future investigations of predictors of durable benefit with dual checkpoint blockade and targeted therapy.","doi":"10.1038/s41467-023-44475-6","published_date":"2024-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38167503/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2024-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'IMspire150 atezolizumab vemurafenib cobimetinib randomized phase 3 progression-free survival overall survival melanoma', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1034 tokens): Summary of key findings across the three sources

Clinical context
- For advanced/metastatic melanoma, immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) can be used regardless of tumor mutational status, while BRAF and MEK inhibitors are effective targeted therapy (TT) options for patients with BRAF V600–mutant disease.
- Given complementary mechanisms and benefits, combinations of ICI with BRAF/MEK inhibitors (“triple therapy”) have been tested.

Evidence on triple therapy vs targeted therapy alone
- Three randomized studies tested an anti–PD-1 or anti–PD-L1 antibody added to BRAF+MEK inhibition versus BRAF+MEK plus placebo.
- Across these, duration of response (DoR) was statistically significantly longer with triple therapy; however, overall survival (OS) and progression-free survival (PFS) were only numerically superior, if at all.
- One triple regimen—atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib—received approval for BRAF-mutant metastatic melanoma.
- The anticipated biological synergy of triple therapy has not been consistently confirmed; observed benefits may reflect statistical rather than clearly demonstrated biological effects.

IMspire150 program (atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib)
- Design: Multicentre, randomized, phase 3, first-line treatment for BRAF V600–mutation–positive unresectable/advanced melanoma (atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib vs placebo + vemurafenib + cobimetinib).
- Primary analysis (2020, Lancet): Demonstrated improved investigator-assessed PFS with the triple regimen compared with control.
  - Citation: Gutzmer et al., 2020, Lancet; DOI: 10.1016/S0140-6736(20)30934-X.
- Subsequent analysis focused on OS with first-line atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib (2023, Lancet Oncology), building on the primary PFS benefit.
  - Citation: Ascierto et al., 2023, Lancet Oncology; DOI: 10.1016/S1470-2045(22)00687-8.
- Commentary and contextualization (2023): The second interim analysis of IMspire150 and broader evidence suggest triple therapy’s advantages over TT alone are most consistent for DoR, with OS/PFS benefits modest or inconsistent; the universal biological synergy remains unproven.
  - Citation: Dummer et al., 2023, J Transl Med; DOI: 10.1186/s12967-023-04391-1.

Therapy sequencing in BRAF-mutant melanoma
- Two prospective trials compared:
  - First-line ICI followed by TT at progression versus
  - First-line TT followed by ICI at progression.
- The Secombit study also tested a “sandwich” approach: 8 weeks of TT → ICI until progression → TT again.
- Findings:
  - Both trials demonstrated a benefit for starting with ICI first line.
  - In Secombit, the “sandwich” strategy produced similar outcomes to first-line ICI.
- Implication: Current data support immunotherapy-based regimens as preferred first-line therapy for BRAF-mutant melanoma; the sandwich approach is a reasonable alternative.

Unresolved questions and clinical considerations
- Whether triple therapy is superior to ICI (mono- or standard ICI-based) regimens remains unanswered and should be judged on both efficacy and safety.
- Given approvals and available data, triple therapy may be considered in selected patients, but routine superiority over ICI-based strategies has not been definitively established.

References (with PubMed links)
- Dummer R, Welti M, Ramelyte E. The role of triple therapy and therapy sequence… (commentary on IMspire150 second interim OS). J Transl Med. 2023. PubMed: 37543586. DOI: 10.1186/s12967-023-04391-1.
- Ascierto PA et al. Overall survival with first-line atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib (IMspire150 OS analysis). Lancet Oncol. 2023. PubMed: 36460017. DOI: 10.1016/S1470-2045(22)00687-8.
- Gutzmer R et al. IMspire150 primary analysis (PFS). Lancet. 2020. PubMed: 32534646. DOI: 10.1016/S0140-6736(20)30934-X.
