- Study focus: Mechanisms of resistance to RAF/MEK inhibitors in B-RAF(V600E) melanoma via MAPK pathway reactivation.
- Methods: Parallel overexpression screen of ~600 kinase and kinase-related ORFs to identify drivers of resistance to a selective RAF inhibitor in B-RAF(V600E) cell lines.
- Key findings:
  - MAP3K8 (COT/Tpl2) identified as a MAPK pathway agonist that confers resistance to RAF inhibition in B-RAF(V600E) melanoma cell lines.
  - COT activates ERK primarily through MEK-dependent mechanisms that bypass RAF signaling.
  - COT expression correlates with:
    - De novo resistance in B-RAF(V600E) cultured cell lines.
    - Acquired resistance in melanoma cells and patient tissues at relapse after treatment with MEK or RAF inhibitors.
- Therapeutic implications:
  - Combinatorial inhibition within the MAPK pathway (e.g., targeting multiple nodes) or direct inhibition of COT kinase activity may reduce MAPK pathway reactivation and overcome resistance.
- Significance: Provides mechanistic insight into MAPK pathway–mediated drug resistance and demonstrates how high-throughput functional screens can reveal alternative druggable targets to guide long-term treatment strategies.
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'Wagle 2014 Cancer Discovery MAP kinase pathway alterations acquired resistance to dabrafenib trametinib MEK2 Q60P melanoma', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"24265154","title":"MAP kinase pathway alterations in BRAF-mutant melanoma patients with acquired resistance to combined RAF/MEK inhibition.","authors":"Wagle N; Van Allen EM; Treacy DJ; Frederick DT; Cooper ZA; Taylor-Weiner A; Rosenberg M; Goetz EM; Sullivan RJ; Farlow DN; Friedrich DC; Anderka K; Perrin D; Johannessen CM; McKenna A; Cibulskis K; Kryukov G; Hodis E; Lawrence DP; Fisher S; Getz G; Gabriel SB; Carter SL; Flaherty KT; Wargo JA; Garraway LA","abstract":"Treatment of BRAF-mutant melanoma with combined dabrafenib and trametinib, which target RAF and the downstream MAP-ERK kinase (MEK)1 and MEK2 kinases, respectively, improves progression-free survival and response rates compared with dabrafenib monotherapy. Mechanisms of clinical resistance to combined RAF/MEK inhibition are unknown. We performed whole-exome sequencing (WES) and whole-transcriptome sequencing (RNA-seq) on pretreatment and drug-resistant tumors from five patients with acquired resistance to dabrafenib/trametinib. In three of these patients, we identified additional mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) pathway alterations in the resistant tumor that were not detected in the pretreatment tumor, including a novel activating mutation in MEK2 (MEK2(Q60P)). MEK2(Q60P) conferred resistance to combined RAF/MEK inhibition in vitro, but remained sensitive to inhibition of the downstream kinase extracellular signal-regulated kinase (ERK). The continued MAPK signaling-based resistance identified in these patients suggests that alternative dosing of current agents, more potent RAF/MEK inhibitors, and/or inhibition of the downstream kinase ERK may be needed for durable control of BRAF-mutant melanoma.","doi":"10.1158/2159-8290.CD-13-0631","published_date":"2014-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24265154/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2014-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
