2025-12-01 10:20:54,884 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Short answer
- No: asteroid flybys do not cause geomagnetic storms or influence solar activity. Geomagnetic storms are driven by solar wind structures from the Sun (coronal mass ejections, CMEs, and high‑speed streams/HSS from coronal holes), not by asteroids.
- This week’s space‑weather drivers were solar: an X1.9 flare on 2025‑12‑01 with an associated fast halo CME near the Sun’s east limb, plus a high‑speed stream on 2025‑11‑28. Several asteroids passed close, but their sizes and masses are far too small, and their distances far too large, to have any measurable geophysical or solar effect.

What’s actually happening this week (with sources)
1) Near‑Earth asteroid flybys (closest and hazardous highlights)
- 2025 WO8 (very small, a few meters)
  - Closest approach: 2025‑11‑29 18:15 UTC at 108,705.861 km from Earth (0.283 lunar distances)
  - Size: 0.003686–0.008242 km (3.7–8.2 m); speed: 26,766.733 km/h; Hazardous: No
  - NASA JPL SBDB: https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=54564341
- 2025 US6 (very small)
  - Closest approach: 2025‑12‑02 06:40 UTC at 159,495.549 km (0.415 lunar distances)
  - Size: 0.001671–0.003736 km (1.7–3.7 m); speed: 6,814.320 km/h; Hazardous: No
  - NASA JPL SBDB: https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=54554706
- 2007 VM184 (Potentially Hazardous Asteroid, but far)
  - Approach: 2025‑12‑01 09:38 UTC at 4,997,973 km; size: 0.164–0.366 km; speed: 72,064 km/h; Hazardous: Yes
  - NASA JPL SBDB: https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=3394543
- 533722 (2014 NE52) (PHA, but far)
  - Approach: 2025‑12‑03 07:43 UTC at 15,835,845 km; size: 0.609–1.362 km; Hazardous: Yes
  - NASA JPL SBDB: https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=2533722
- 297418 (2000 SP43) (PHA, but far)
  - Approach: 2025‑12‑08 12:33 UTC at 27,894,399 km; size: 0.525–1.175 km; Hazardous: Yes
  - NASA JPL SBDB: https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=2297418

2) Solar activity and space‑weather drivers
- X1.9 solar flare (on‑disk near E limb) and fast halo CME
  - Flare: Begin 2025‑12‑01 02:27 UTC; Peak 02:49; End 03:05; Class X1.9; location ~N23E90
  - CME: First seen 2025‑12‑01 02:48 UTC; “halo” morphology; DONKI analysis speed 1,837 km/s; classification “On” (source on visible disk)
  - NASA DONKI CME event page (includes instrument details): https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43155/-1
  - DONKI flare listing (ID 2025‑12‑01T02:27:00‑FLR‑001) appears in DONKI output and notifications (e.g., 20251201‑AL‑002; X1.9 flare)
- High‑speed stream (HSS) from a coronal hole
  - Event time: 2025‑11‑28 14:57 UTC
  - NASA DONKI HSS: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/43094/-1
  - Another HSS in the prior week: 2025‑11‑24 07:23 UTC: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/42978/-1
- Model/notification context
  - Several WSA‑Enlil runs around 2025‑11‑29–30 focused on impacts to Parker Solar Probe; a DONKI CME notification on 2025‑12‑01 references Mars (not Earth). No DONKI GST notifications were retrieved in this window (tool connection issues), and the available DONKI notes emphasize non‑Earth targets for modeled impacts.

Why asteroids cannot trigger geomagnetic storms or change solar activity (with numbers)
- Geomagnetic storm drivers are well established:
  - Interplanetary CMEs (ICMEs) and their sheaths, and high‑speed streams/CIRs (corotating interaction regions) from coronal holes are the primary causes of storms at Earth.
  - Representative peer‑reviewed sources:
    - Gonzalez et al. (1994), “What is a geomagnetic storm?” JGR 99(A4), 5771–5792. doi:10.1029/93JA02867
    - Tsurutani et al. (2006), “Corotating solar wind streams and recurrent geomagnetic activity,” JGR 111, A07S01. doi:10.1029/2005JA011273
    - Richardson & Cane (2012), “Near‑Earth ICMEs during solar cycle 23,” Solar Physics 281, 187–222. doi:10.1007/s11207-012-0094-0
- There is no physical coupling for asteroids to drive space weather:
  - They have no solar‑wind outflow or magnetic structures to interact with Earth’s magnetosphere.
  - Their gravity at Earth’s distance is negligible. Two examples:
    - 2025 WO8 (3.7–8.2 m diameter) at 108,706 km: assuming a rocky 6 m body (~3×10^5 kg), the gravitational acceleration at Earth from this asteroid is a ≈ GM/R^2 ≲ 4×10^−21 m/s^2. Lunar tidal acceleration at Earth is ~10^−6 m/s^2. Ratio ≲ 10^−15.
    - 2007 VM184 (~0.25 km assumed diameter; ~2×10^10 kg) at 4.998×10^6 km (4.998×10^9 m): a ≈ 5×10^−20 m/s^2, still ~10^−14 of the Moon’s tidal effect.
  - These contributions are many orders of magnitude too small to affect Earth’s magnetosphere, atmosphere, or the Sun.

Putting this week’s facts together
- We observed very close but tiny asteroid flybys (WO8 at 0.283 LD; US6 at 0.415 LD), plus some larger PHAs millions to tens of millions of kilometers away. None of these can influence solar activity or geomagnetic conditions.
- The notable geospace drivers were solar: an X1.9 flare and fast halo CME near E90 on 2025‑12‑01, and an HSS on 2025‑11‑28. DONKI notifications and modeling during this period emphasized impacts to spacecraft (e.g., Parker Solar Probe) and Mars, not Earth, and we retrieved no GST notifications for Earth in this window.
- Therefore, any geomagnetic variability around these dates would be due to solar wind conditions (CMEs/HSS), not asteroids.

Where to verify and monitor
- Asteroid approach data (object pages with approach tables)
  - 2025 WO8: https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=54564341
  - 2025 US6: https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=54554706
  - 2007 VM184: https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=3394543
  - 2014 NE52: https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=2533722
  - 2000 SP43: https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=2297418
- Solar events and modeling
  - CME 2025‑12‑01 (halo; 1,837 km/s): https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43155/-1
  - HSS 2025‑11‑28: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/43094/-1
  - DONKI notifications (flare/CME IDs cited above): https://kauai.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/
- Geomagnetic indices for confirmation (independent of DONKI)
  - NOAA SWPC Kp index (real time): https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/products/planetary-k-index
  - Kyoto WDC Dst index: https://wdc.kugi.kyoto-u.ac.jp/dstdir/
- Background on what causes geomagnetic storms
  - Gonzalez et al. 1994 (JGR), doi:10.1029/93JA02867
  - Tsurutani et al. 2006 (JGR), doi:10.1029/2005JA011273
  - Richardson & Cane 2012 (Solar Physics), doi:10.1007/s11207-012-0094-0

Notes on data gaps
- The DONKI API intermittently returned “connection error” for some products (e.g., GST listings). Nonetheless, the retrieved DONKI event records (flare/CME/HSS), JPL asteroid data, and absence of any Earth‑directed storm notifications are sufficient to conclude that current and recent space‑weather activity is solar‑driven and unrelated to asteroid flybys.

Bottom line you can cite
- The closest asteroid passes this week were 2025 WO8 at 108,706 km and 2025 US6 at 159,496 km; both are meter‑class bodies, non‑hazardous, and physically incapable of influencing solar activity or Earth’s magnetosphere (gravitational effects ≲10^−14–10^−15 of the Moon’s tides).
- Geomagnetic storms arise from solar CMEs and high‑speed streams (see cited literature). Around the time in question, NASA DONKI logged an X1.9 flare with a 1,837 km/s halo CME on 2025‑12‑01 (near E90; modeling/notifications emphasized non‑Earth targets) and an HSS on 2025‑11‑28. No DONKI geomagnetic storm notifications were retrieved for Earth in this period.
