Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'geomagnetic storms drivers CME high-speed streams review', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5009 tokens): SUMMARIZED CONTENT:
- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (cs.CV). Authors: Zhizhou Zhong et al.
  - Problem: Audio-driven multi-person talking video generation is constrained by scarce diverse multi-person data and difficulty in coordinating multiple identities.
  - Approach: Extensible multi-stream Diffusion Transformer with a novel identity-aware attention that iteratively processes identity–audio pairs, enabling arbitrary scaling of drivable identities. Training relies primarily on single-person videos to learn multi-person speaking patterns, with limited real multi-person clips for interactivity refinement.
  - Contributions: Identity-aware attention extension to DiT; scalable multi-person driving; training pipeline that minimizes multi-person data needs; new metric and dataset to evaluate naturalness and interactivity.
  - Results: Strong lip sync, high visual quality, and natural interactivity; favorable trade-off between data cost and identity scalability.

- A retrospective on the 2025 Atlantic hurricane season (physics.ao-ph). Author: Charles W. Powell
  - Overview: Above-average overall activity with extended quiet periods and three clusters of storms despite generally unfavorable large-scale conditions and no La Niña.
  - Drivers: Short-term variability (periods of weak shear, episodic equatorial wave forcing) enabled clusters; above-average SSTs fueled intensification when storms formed.
  - Outcomes: Five hurricanes formed; three reached Category 5 (Erin, Humberto, Melissa).

- ML Researchers Support Openness in Peer Review But Are Concerned About Resubmission Bias (cs.DL). Authors: Vishisht Rao et al.
  - Survey: 2,385 ML peer-review participants.
  - Preferences: >80% support releasing reviews for accepted papers and allowing public comments; only 27.1% support releasing rejected manuscripts.
  - Perceived benefits: Improved public understanding (75.3%), reviewer education (57.8%), increased fairness (56.6%), stronger incentives for high-quality reviews (48.0%).
  - Concerns: Resubmission bias is top issue (ranked most impactful by 41%; mentioned in >50% of free responses); fear of reviewer de-anonymization (33.2%); potential commenting abuse.
  - AI aspects: Open policies seen as deterring “AI slop” submissions (71.9%) and AI-generated reviews (38.9%). Respondents split on venues generating official AI reviews (56.0% opposed, 44.0% supportive).
  - Empirical analysis: AI-annotated 4,244 reviews (ICLR fully open vs NeurIPS partially open). ICLR shows higher correctness (small effect) and completeness (very small effect), both statistically significant; no significant difference in substantiation.
  - Resources: Dataset released at https://github.com/justinpayan/OpenReviewAnalysis.

- The EBLM Project XVIII. 3D Obliquities of Five Low-Mass Eclipsing Binaries (astro-ph.SR; astro-ph.EP). Authors: Becca Spejcher et al.
  - Context: Formation of tight (sub-AU) stellar binaries remains puzzling; obliquity (spin–orbit angle) is a formation clue.
  - Method: Rossiter–McLaughlin measurements for five EBLM systems (J0239-20, J0941-31, 1037-25, 1141-37, J2025-45) with M-dwarf companions and F/G primaries; combined CORALIE/HARPS spectroscopy with TESS photometry (primary and secondary eclipses).
  - Findings: Sky-projected obliquities appear aligned, yet true 3D misalignments ψ ≈ 5–20°. Primaries straddle the Kraft break (~6250 K). M-dwarf masses and radii measured to <3% precision; all but J0941-31 show inflated radii (>5σ above models).

- Joint Resource Allocation to Transparently Integrate 5G TDD Uplink with Time-Aware TSN (cs.NI). Authors: Laura Becker et al.
  - Problem: Deterministic end-to-end scheduling across 5G–TSN requires guaranteed per-class Bridge Delays (BD); traditional uplink schedulers miss deadlines.
  - Solution: Heterogeneous radio resource scheduler combining static pre-allocation for time-sensitive periodic streams (aligned with TSN Time-Aware Shape


