Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'geomagnetic storms causes review coronal mass ejections corotating interaction regions high-speed streams', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4755 tokens): - Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (cs.CV). Rasheed et al. Introduces “Interactive Video Reasoning,” enabling models to think with videos via iterative visual actions (Chain of Manipulations, CoM). Trains on Video-CoM Instruct (18K multi-step manipulation instructions). Optimizes manipulation policy with GRPO using step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards), improving groundedness and consistency. Achieves +3.6% average over recent SOTA across nine video reasoning benchmarks using only 25K SFT and 3K GRPO samples. Ablations show reasoning-aware rewards boost accuracy and interpretability. Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

- Thinking by Doing: Efficient World Model Reasoning via Multi-turn Interaction (cs.AI). Shu et al. Propose WMAct to internalize world models through active interaction without rigid, structured reasoning. Key mechanisms: (1) reward rescaling (based on action efficacy) to reduce redundancy and encourage purposeful interaction; (2) interaction frequency annealing to progressively cap interaction turns, forcing internalization. On Sokoban, Maze, Taxi, resolves tasks in a single turn that previously needed multiple interactions and shows strong transferability with improved benchmark performance.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (cs.CV). Zhong et al. Multi-person, audio-driven video generation with an extensible multi-stream architecture. Adds identity-aware attention to a Diffusion Transformer, iteratively processing identity–audio pairs for arbitrary scaling of identities. Training relies mainly on single-person videos to learn multi-person speaking patterns, refining interactivity with few real multi-person clips. Contributes a metric and dataset to assess naturalness/interactivity. Demonstrates strong lip-sync, visual quality, and natural interactivity with favorable data cost–scalability tradeoff.

- Spectral analysis of the Koopman operator recovers Hamiltonian parameters in open quantum systems (quant-ph; math-ph). Pérez-García et al. Use Koopman operator spectrum via multichannel HAVOK (mHAVOK) to infer Hamiltonian parameters from first-moment dynamics of an open 2D quantum harmonic oscillator. Recovers oscillation frequencies, damping, Kerr shifts, Jaynes–Cummings coupling, and time-dependent modulation; most within 5% of true values. Outperforms Fourier and matrix-pencil under strong dissipation; provides theoretical justification.

- Gravitational Waves from Confining Dark Sectors with Self-Consistent Effective Potentials (hep-ph). Houtz et al. Predict GW signals from confinement-induced phase transitions in hidden SU(N) (F light flavors), imposing perturbativity/unitarity to ensure EFT reliability. Include Polyakov-loop–improved finite-T potentials for N=3,4 with approximate mediating effects. Compute GW spectra and viable parameter space; theoretical consistency constraints significantly diminish detectable stochastic backgrounds, challenging prospects even for future detectors.

- Schwinger effect with backreaction in 1+1D massive QED with a strong external field (hep-th; cond-mat.other; gr-qc). Gralla & Mizuno. Bosonized massive QED with cosine interaction; strong-field limit permits perturbative expansion in fermion mass m. The vacuum expectation of the electric field at O(m) satisfies a classical nonlinear PDE (related to sine-Gordon). Predicts dissipation-free plasma-like oscillations; analytic plasma frequency derived. Semiclassical backreaction misses the O(m) frequency shift.

- New Particles at the Z-Pole: Tera-Z factories as discovery and precision machines (hep-ph; hep-ex). Drewes et al. Analytical estimates for long-lived particle yields vs number of Z’s and detector size at future Tera-Z colliders (FCC-ee, CEPC, LEP3, LEP-Z). Provide rapid sensitivity/precision projections; examples: heavy neutral leptons and axion-like particles could be produced in millions/billions
