2025-12-01 10:28:16,652 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Short answer: For a one‑week California camping trip this winter (early December), the strongest, most dependable choices—based on open facilities, camping supply, and current NPS alerts—are Joshua Tree, Death Valley (with conditions caveat), Pinnacles, and Yosemite Valley (lower elevations only). Redwood also works if you’re fine with rain and coastal conditions. Sequoia–Kings Canyon and Lassen have significant seasonal road closures that limit access.

Details with concrete data, campgrounds, visitor centers, and current alerts (all links are to official NPS or Recreation.gov sources)

1) Joshua Tree National Park (best overall winter weather + many open campgrounds)
- Why now: Typical winter days ~60°F with freezing nights; usually dry and clear; stargazing is excellent. Source: NPS park page, Weather section https://www.nps.gov/jotr/index.htm
- Campgrounds (selected, with site counts/fees/booking rules)
  - Black Rock: 99 sites; reservations required year‑round; $35/night; water, flush toilets, dump station; RVs to 35 ft; ADA site #61. Reserve up to 6 months ahead via Recreation.gov. Sources: NPS campground summary; Rec.gov https://www.recreation.gov/camping/campgrounds/232447 • NPS VC page (ADA info): https://www.nps.gov/jotr/planyourvisit/visitorcenters.htm
  - Cottonwood: 62 sites; reservations required year‑round; $35/night; water, flush toilets, dump station; RVs/trailers to 35 ft. Note: Flush toilets currently out of service at the Cottonwood Visitor Center; plan accordingly. Source: NPS campground summary + VC status https://www.nps.gov/jotr/planyourvisit/visitorcenters.htm
  - Belle: 18 sites; first‑come, first‑served; $25/night; vault toilets; no water; RVs up to 35 ft; bring all water. Source: NPS campground summary https://www.nps.gov/jotr/index.htm (Campgrounds)
- Visitor centers and typical winter hours
  - Joshua Tree Visitor Center (town): daily 7:30 am–5:00 pm. Source: NPS https://www.nps.gov/jotr/planyourvisit/visitorcenters.htm
  - Twentynine Palms (main VC): daily 8:00 am–5:00 pm. Source: same link above
  - Cottonwood VC: daily 8:30 am–4:00 pm (note on restrooms). Source: same link above
- Active alerts/issues
  - Queen Valley Complex (Bighorn Sheep Rd, Queen Valley Rd, O’dell Rd) closed due to flooding (updated 2025‑11‑16). Oasis of Mara Trail closed due to flood damage (2025‑09‑28). NPS cautions about inaccurate third‑party hiking apps—use the official NPS app. Source: NPS Alerts https://www.nps.gov/jotr/index.htm (Alerts) and https://www.nps.gov/subjects/digital/nps-apps.htm

2) Death Valley National Park (premier winter desert camping; check flood-related closures)
- Why now: Winter has cool days/chilly nights and clear skies; summer heat is extreme. Source: NPS park page https://www.nps.gov/deva/index.htm
- Campgrounds (selected)
  - Furnace Creek: 136 sites; reservations accepted Oct 15–Apr 15 (5 days to 6 months in advance); standard $30/night; full‑hookup sites $44; group sites $40–$60; water, flush toilets, dump station; ADA sites listed (e.g., #22, #147; hookups + ADA #49, #77). Reserve via Recreation.gov (ID 232496). Sources: NPS CG page https://www.nps.gov/deva/planyourvisit/furnace-creek-campground.htm • Rec.gov https://www.recreation.gov/camping/campgrounds/232496
  - Emigrant (tent‑only, 10 sites) is closed indefinitely due to flood damage to the water system (closure began late Aug 2025). Source: NPS CG status https://www.nps.gov/deva/planyourvisit/emigrant-campground.htm
- Visitor centers
  - Furnace Creek VC: open daily 8:00 am–5:00 pm. Source: NPS VC page https://www.nps.gov/deva/planyourvisit/places-to-go.htm
  - Stovepipe Wells Ranger Station: typically 10:00 am–4:00 pm (call to confirm). Source: same link above
- Active alerts/issues
  - “Many roads closed due to flooding on 11/18/2025.” The NPS maintains an updated conditions list covering roads and campgrounds; check immediately before travel. Source: NPS Conditions (updated 2025‑11‑19) https://www.nps.gov/deva/planyourvisit/conditions.htm
  - Fuel note: Panamint Springs gas available 7:00 am–9:30 pm; Furnace Creek and Stovepipe Wells offer 24‑hour pumps. Source: NPS Alerts https://www.nps.gov/deva/index.htm (Alerts)

3) Pinnacles National Park (excellent shoulder‑season camping; compact park with amenities)
- Why now: Mild winter days; outstanding hikes among volcanic spires; condor viewing; West entrance has limited hours but East entrance is 24/7. Source: NPS park page https://www.nps.gov/pinn/index.htm
- Campground
  - Pinnacles Campground: 134 total sites (83 tent‑only, 30 RV‑only, 21 group); all reservable (no FCFS); tent $43, RV $59, group $110; showers, laundry, dump station, camp store, Wi‑Fi/cell; pool open mid‑April–Sept. Reserve via Recreation.gov (ID 234015). Sources: NPS CG page summary and operator site https://www.visitpinnacles.com/ • Rec.gov https://www.recreation.gov/camping/campgrounds/234015
- Visitor facilities
  - Bear Gulch Nature Center (east side): typically 9:00 am–4:00 pm, closed Thu–Fri. West Pinnacles Visitor Contact Station: often 9:00 am–3:00 pm (may close seasonally). Park Store in campground: daily 9:00 am–4:00 pm. Source: NPS VC listings https://www.nps.gov/pinn/planyourvisit/places-to-go.htm
- Active alerts/issues
  - High crowds & parking capacity limits, especially 8:00 am–3:00 pm on weekends—rangers stop cars when lots are full. Source: NPS Alert https://www.nps.gov/pinn/planyourvisit/hours.htm

4) Yosemite National Park (lower‑elevation winter access; major high‑country roads closed)
- Why now: Yosemite Valley is open year‑round; iconic scenery with winter conditions. However, Tioga Road and Glacier Point Road close in winter; some trail work closures in the Mist/JMT area occur on weekdays. Source: NPS park page https://www.nps.gov/yose/index.htm
- Campgrounds (selected)
  - Camp 4: 61 walk‑in tent sites; $10 per person/night; open year‑round. In 2025: Apr 18–Nov 15 by reservation (released 1 week ahead); otherwise FCFS/self‑register. No pets; no RVs. Sources: NPS Camp 4 page https://www.nps.gov/yose/planyourvisit/camp4.htm • Rec.gov listing https://www.recreation.gov/camping/campgrounds/10004152
  - Bridalveil Creek: 115 sites; typically mid‑July–early Sept; $36; reservations required and released 14 days in advance at 7 am PT. Glacier Point Road access is closed in winter. Source: NPS page https://www.nps.gov/yose/planyourvisit/bvcamp.htm • Rec.gov https://www.recreation.gov/camping/campgrounds/232453
  - Crane Flat: 151 sites; typically July–mid‑Oct; $36; reservations required, released 14 days in advance. Source: Rec.gov https://www.recreation.gov/camping/campgrounds/232452
- Visitor centers (selected)
  - Yosemite Valley Welcome Center: year‑round, daily 9:00 am–5:00 pm. Source: NPS https://www.nps.gov/yose/planyourvisit/visitorcenters.htm
  - Wawona, Tuolumne Meadows, and Big Oak Flat information stations operate seasonally (late spring–early fall). Source: same link above
- Active alerts/issues
  - Tioga Road (Hwy 120 through park) and Glacier Point Road temporarily closed due to snow (updated 2025‑11‑21). Call 209‑372‑0200 (then 1, 1) for road conditions. Source: NPS alert https://www.nps.gov/yose/planyourvisit/wroads.htm
  - Weekday trail closures for blasting/repair in the Vernal/Nevada Falls area (Mist Trail/JMT/Panorama segments) (updated 2025‑11‑26). Source: NPS alert on park site (see Yosemite alerts page)

5) Redwood National and State Parks (coastal redwoods; open year‑round; expect rain)
- Why now: Lush winter forests and open campgrounds; temperatures often mid‑40s to mid‑60s°F; heavy rain likely. Source: NPS park page https://www.nps.gov/redw/index.htm
- Developed campgrounds (CA State Parks, reserved via ReserveCalifornia)
  - Elk Prairie: 75 sites; flush toilets, free hot showers, water; RVs ≤27 ft, trailers ≤24 ft. Source: NPS page https://www.nps.gov/redw/planyourvisit/elk-prairie-campground.htm
  - Gold Bluffs Beach: 26 sites; flush toilets, free hot showers, water; access via narrow dirt road; RVs ≤24 ft (not advised); trailers prohibited. Source: NPS page https://www.nps.gov/redw/planyourvisit/gold-bluffs-beach-campground.htm
  - Jedediah Smith: 86 sites; flush toilets, coin showers, water; RVs ≤25 ft, trailers ≤21 ft. Source: NPS page https://www.nps.gov/redw/planyourvisit/jedediah-smith-campground.htm
  - Reservations/Fees: ReserveCalifornia; typical $35/night; some pass discounts apply. Source: NPS camping overview https://www.nps.gov/redw/planyourvisit/camping-in-the-redwoods.htm • ReserveCalifornia https://www.reservecalifornia.com/
- Visitor centers (selected)
  - Thomas H. Kuchel VC (southern, on the beach): winter 10:00 am–4:00 pm. Prairie Creek VC: winter Wed–Sun 10:00 am–4:00 pm. Hiouchi VC: winter 10:00 am–4:00 pm, closed Wed/Thu. Source: NPS VC page https://www.nps.gov/redw/planyourvisit/visitorcenters.htm
- Active alerts/issues
  - Newton B. Drury Scenic Parkway closed to vehicles Dec 1–3 for maintenance; open to walking/biking. Source: NPS alert (updated 2025‑11‑24) on REDW alerts page

Parks to de‑prioritize for early December (due to closures)

- Sequoia & Kings Canyon (SEKI): Key winter closures are in effect—Mineral King Road, Hwy 180 between Grant Grove and Cedar Grove, Panoramic Point; several small roads (e.g., South Fork, Redwood Canyon) remain closed from storm damage. Azalea Campground is open year‑round (110 sites; $32/night; winter operation = ~20 FCFS sites). Sources: NPS Alerts (updated 2025‑11‑23 & 2025‑09‑11) https://www.nps.gov/seki/planyourvisit/road-conditions.htm • NPS CG page https://www.nps.gov/seki/planyourvisit/azalea.htm
- Lassen Volcanic: Lassen Park Highway (Hwy 89) is closed for the snow season; additional cautions for impassable roads (Butte Lake, Juniper Lake, Warner Valley). Backcountry camping requires a paid wilderness permit ($6 admin + $5/person). Butte Lake CG is reservation‑only when open (101 sites, $22 when water on). Sources: NPS Alerts (updated 2025‑11‑17, 2025‑11‑19) https://www.nps.gov/lavo/planyourvisit/weather.htm • Backcountry permit https://www.recreation.gov/permits/4675334 • Butte Lake CG https://www.recreation.gov/camping/campgrounds/234156

Optional/advanced: Channel Islands (boat‑in only; great if seas allow)
- Camping is primitive and by reservation only at island campgrounds (typical $15/site/night; Del Norte backcountry on Santa Cruz has 4 sites; Anacapa has 7 sites but requires climbing 157 stairs; potable water only at Scorpion Campground on Santa Cruz). Sources: NPS camping pages https://www.nps.gov/chis/planyourvisit/camping.htm • Rec.gov listings (search per island)
- Visitor center (mainland): Ventura Harbor, daily 8:30 am–5:00 pm; fully accessible. Source: NPS VC page https://www.nps.gov/chis/planyourvisit/visitorcenters.htm
- Alerts: Santa Rosa Island has temporary beach/dune closures near China Camp/Cluster Point (updated 2024‑02‑22); Santa Barbara Island dock remains closed—landings via adjacent rocky ledge (long‑term). Sources: NPS Alerts https://www.nps.gov/chis/planyourvisit/conditions.htm and linked backcountry beach page

How I’d pick for a one‑week December trip (two evidence‑based options)

- Warm desert week (lowest closure risk):
  - 3–4 nights Joshua Tree (Black Rock or Cottonwood for water/flush toilets; Belle if you want FCFS solitude and you’ll haul water).
  - 3–4 nights Death Valley centered at Furnace Creek (reserve if within Oct 15–Apr 15; check road conditions page first due to November flood closures). Sources above.

- Central CA week (cooler, but iconic scenery):
  - 3–4 nights Pinnacles (full‑service campground; plan early start on weekends due to capacity alerts).
  - 3–4 nights Yosemite Valley (Camp 4 walk‑in sites; expect winter driving restrictions; Tioga/Glacier Point closed). Sources above.

Trip‑critical links you can check just before departure
- Park road/conditions pages:
  - Yosemite roads/conditions: https://www.nps.gov/yose/planyourvisit/wroads.htm or call 209‑372‑0200 (then 1, 1)
  - Death Valley conditions (roads/campgrounds): https://www.nps.gov/deva/planyourvisit/conditions.htm
  - SEKI road conditions: https://www.nps.gov/seki/planyourvisit/road-conditions.htm
  - Lassen winter/roads: https://www.nps.gov/lavo/planyourvisit/weather.htm
  - Redwood alerts: https://www.nps.gov/redw/planyourvisit/conditions.htm
- Campground reservations:
  - Recreation.gov (NPS): https://www.recreation.gov/
  - ReserveCalifornia (Redwood SP campgrounds): https://www.reservecalifornia.com/

If you tell me your exact dates and whether you want desert warmth vs. forest/coast scenery, I’ll turn this into a bookable 7‑day plan with specific sites to target as they open on Recreation.gov/ReserveCalifornia.
