Tool `National Parks:getParkDetails` with Parameter {'parkCode': 'deva'} on National Parks succeeded. Result (summarized from 2751 tokens): Death Valley National Park (code: deva)
- URL: https://www.nps.gov/deva/index.htm
- Designation: National Park
- States: CA, NV
- Description: Below-sea-level basin known for extremes—steady drought and record summer heat—balanced by contrasts like snow-capped peaks, rare wildflower blooms after rain, and lush oases that support diverse life.

Weather
- Spring: Most popular; warm, sunny; potential wildflowers.
- Summer: Starts early; scorching heat by May.
- Autumn: Arrives late October; warm, pleasant, clear skies.
- Winter: Cool days, chilly nights; rare rain; snow on high peaks; great low-angle light for exploring.

Directions
- Multiple entrances; guidance at: http://www.nps.gov/deva/planyourvisit/directions.htm

Location
- Latitude/Longitude: 36.48753731, -117.134395
- Address: PO Box 579, Death Valley, CA 92328

Contacts
- Phone: 760-786-3200
- Email: deva_information@nps.gov

Entrance Fees (7-day permits unless noted)
- Entrance – Private Vehicle: $30.00. All persons in one private, non-commercial vehicle (car/truck/van/RV/aircraft) may re-enter during the period.
- Entrance – Motorcycle: $25.00. One motorcycle; re-entry permitted.
- Entrance – Per Person: $15.00. For one individual on foot or bicycle; re-entry permitted.
- Commercial Entrance – Per Person: $10.00 (plus $25 base for 1–6 passenger vehicle). Additional permits required for commercial groups, special uses (weddings, memorials, group events), commercial film/photography, research, etc.
- Commercial Entrance – Sedan: $25.00 (1–6 passenger vehicle) + $10 per person. Additional permits may be required as above.
- Commercial Entrance – Van: $75.00 (7–15 passenger vehicle). Additional permits may be required as above.
- Commercial Entrance – Mini-bus: $100.00 (16–25 passenger vehicle). Additional permits may be required as above.
- Commercial Entrance – Motor Coach: $200.00 (26+ passengers). Additional permits may be required as above.
- Entrance – Non-commercial Groups: $15.00 per person for vehicles with capacity of 16+ persons; not to exceed $200 total.
- Entrance – Education/Academic Groups: $0.00. Fee waiver available for approved national/international academic institutions for educational/scientific visits using park resources to support those purposes; submit waiver at least one month before visit.

Entrance Passes
- Annual Entrance – Park: $55.00. All persons with pass holder in one private, non-commercial vehicle or one motorcycle; valid 12 months from month of purchase.

Operating Hours
- Park is open daily, all year.
- Standard hours: Monday–Sunday: All Day.

Topics
- Archeology; Asian American Heritage; Incarceration; Japanese-American Internment; Industry; Mining; Native American Heritage; Westward Expansion; The Gold Rush; Women’s History; Animals; Birds; Fish; Tortoises and Turtles; Dunes; Foothills, Plains and Valleys; Fossils and Paleontology; Geology; Mountains; Night Sky; Astronomy; Scenic Views; Trails; Unique Species; Endangered; Wilderness.

Activities
- Astronomy, Stargazing
- Biking: Mountain Biking, Road Biking
- Camping: Backcountry, Car/Front Country
- Canyoneering
- Food
- Flying
- Golf
- Guided Tours
- Hiking: Backcountry, Front-Country
- Horse Trekking
- Living History: First Person Interpretation
- Junior Ranger Program
- Wildlife Watching: Birdwatching
- Park Film
- Museum Exhibits
- Shopping: Bookstore/Park Store, Gift Shop/Souvenirs

Highlights
- Zabriskie Point: Iconic sunrise/sunset views over badlands.
- Badwater Basin: Lowest point in North America at -282 feet; expansive salt flats.
- Dantes View: Panoramic vistas ~1 mile above valley salt flats.
- Higher elevations host alpine ecosystems with cooler temperatures.
- Wildrose Charcoal Kilns: Well-preserved 19th-century kilns.
- Wildflower “superbloom” can occur roughly once a decade with timely rains.
- Summers are extremely hot; temperatures above 120°F are regularly recorded for up to six months of the year.
- Numerous narrow, winding canyons to explore.

Images (subjects
Tool `National Parks:getCampgrounds` with Parameter {'parkCode': 'deva', 'limit': 50} on National Parks succeeded. Result (summarized from 28154 tokens): Summary of listed Death Valley NP (park code: deva) campgrounds

Emigrant Campground
- Status: Closed indefinitely (since late Aug 2025) due to substantial flood damage to a 5-mile water pipe/water system; no repair ETA.
- Type/elevation: Small, first-come, tent-only campground at 2,100 ft; 10 sites; free (park entry fee still required).
- Location: Next to CA-190, SW of Stovepipe Wells Ranger Station. Lat/Long: 36.49652164975486, -117.22707243634392.
- Rules: Tents only. Stay limit 30 days. Checkout 12 pm. No site saving. Max: 1 RV w/tow, 2 cars, or 4 motorcycles; 8 people/site. Fires/charcoal prohibited; gas stoves OK. Pets leashed (≤6 ft), not unattended; developed areas/roads only. Secure food/trash; don’t feed wildlife.
- Accessibility: Rough/rocky gravel surface; flush-toilet building 270 ft down dirt path with 2 steps. Not RV/trailer-friendly.
- Amenities (note: facilities closed with the campground): previously listed flush toilets, potable water, trash, etc.
- Hours: Closed all week.
- Info: nps.gov/deva/planyourvisit/emigrant-campground.htm; Phone 760-786-3200; Email deva_information@nps.gov.

Eureka Dunes Campground (Primitive)
- Type/elevation: Remote dry camp at base of CA’s tallest dunes (2,880 ft); protects endemic plants. High-clearance vehicles required; stay on roads; tread lightly.
- Availability/fees: First-come; free (park entry fee/pass required). Total sites 7 (5 shown as FCFS).
- Amenities: Vault toilets (year-round); no water.
- Access: Unpaved; 4WD/high clearance recommended.
- Rules: Max 8 people and 2 vehicles/site. Charcoal fires only in NPS metal fire pits; stoves/grills must be gas-burning. Pets leashed (≤6 ft), max 4 per site, not unattended; pack-out cleanup. Keep campsite clean; don’t feed wildlife. No driving on dunes/off-road.
- Seasonal: Open year-round; summer camping not recommended (heat).
- Location: Lat/Long 37.112, -117.6809.
- Info: nps.gov/deva/planyourvisit/eureka-dunes-campground-primitive.htm; Phone 760-786-3200; Email Deva_Information@nps.gov.

Furnace Creek Campground
- Type/elevation: Only NPS campground in the park taking reservations; near Visitor Center at -190 ft. Full RV hookups and dry RV/tent sites.
- Availability: Reservations accepted Oct 15–Apr 15 (book 5 days–6 months ahead via Recreation.gov or 877-444-6777). Apr 16–Oct 14: reduced to 41 sites, first-come. NPS may hold reserved sites for management needs.
- Sites/fees: 136 sites (all reservable during reservation season). Standard sites $30; Full hookups $44 ($30 + $14 utility fee; utility not discounted). Group sites: #3–5 for 9–15 people, up to 4 vehicles ($40); #1–2 for 9–40 people, up to 10 vehicles ($60). Senior/Access discounts apply to standard site fee only.
- Campsite types: 5 group; 45 tent-only; 18 electrical hookups; 35 walk-in.
- Amenities: Flush toilets (year-round), potable water (year-round), dump station, camp store, trash/recycling, staff host, cell service; no showers.
- Rules: Generator hours 7 am–7 pm (not unattended). Quiet hours 10 pm–7 am. Stay limit 14 days. No site saving. Checkout 12 pm. Fires only in NPS grates; no wood gathering; don’t leave fires unattended. Wastewater only at dump station; no washing at spigots/restrooms. Pets leashed (≤6 ft), developed areas/roads only; not unattended. Secure food/trash; don’t feed wildlife.
- Weather: Often too hot mid-Apr–mid-Oct; nights may stay above 90°F. Nov–Mar pleasant.
- Accessibility: Generally level/ADA-friendly except tent walk-in loop #116–146. ADA sites: #22, #147 (modified amenities); hookups + ADA: #49, #77; ADA-accessible: #84, #115, #149; ADA-accessible with hookups: #74.
- Location: Lat/Long 36.465754151086664, -116.86815754448372.
- Hours: Open year-round; some loops close in summer.
- Info: nps.gov/deva/planyourvisit/furnace-creek-campground.htm; Reservations: recreation.gov/camping/campgrounds/232496; Phone 760-786-3203; Email deva_information@nps.gov.

Homestake Campground (Primitive)
- Type/elevation: Extremely remote dry camp
