Tool `National Parks:getParkDetails` with Parameter {'parkCode': 'redw'} on National Parks succeeded. Result (summarized from 3639 tokens): - Name: Redwood National and State Parks (code: redw)
- Designation: National and State Parks
- Website: https://www.nps.gov/redw/index.htm
- Description: Home to the world’s tallest trees, with landscapes from open prairies and oak woodlands to pristine rivers and rugged coastline. This ancestral homeland has supported people for thousands of years. Managed jointly by the National Park Service and California State Parks to protect and restore the area for enjoyment, learning, and inspiration.
- State: CA

Location
- Coordinates: 41.37237268, -124.0318129
- Address: 1111 Second Street, Crescent City, CA 95531

Weather
- Generally cool and damp; dress in layers and expect wet conditions.
- Typical temps year-round: mid-40s°F (7°C) to mid-60s°F (18°C); summer highs can occasionally reach low 70s°F (20°C).
- Summer is often foggy; winters are cooler with considerable rain.
- Rainfall (Oct–Apr): averages 60–80 inches across the region.

Directions
- Northernmost coastal California, near the Oregon border; park spans ~60 miles with four visitor centers north-to-south.
- Driving: ~6–7 hours (325 mi) north of San Francisco; ~6 hours (330 mi) south of Portland, OR; ~4 hours (170 mi) west of Redding, CA.
- Directions: http://www.nps.gov/redw/planyourvisit/directions.htm

Contacts
- Phone (Voice): 707-464-6101
- Fax: 707-464-1812
- Email: redw_info@nps.gov

Fees/Passes
- Entrance fees: none listed
- Entrance passes: none listed

Operating Hours
- Park, roads, trails, and public access: open year-round (daily, all day).
- Some campgrounds and visitor centers may be closed or have reduced hours in the off-season (Oct–May).

Topics (themes)
- Human history: Colonization and Settlement; Westward Expansion; Homesteading; Social Movements; Conservation Movement; Native American Heritage; Transportation (Roads, Routes, and Highways).
- Industry: Farming and Agriculture; Ranches; Industry; Mills; Logging and Timber Cutting; Maritime; Coastal Defenses.
- Conflicts and events: Tragic Events (Massacres); Wars and Conflicts (Tribal Conflicts; Indian and Frontier Wars).
- Natural resources/ecosystems: Animals (Birds; Cats—wild; Elk; Fish); Climate Change; Fire; Forests and Woodlands (Coniferous); Geology; Groundwater; Grasslands (Prairies); Oceans (Whales); River and Riparian; Watersheds (Headwaters); Wilderness; Scenic Views; Unique Species (Rare; Endangered).

Activities
- Arts and Culture (Cultural Demonstrations)
- Auto/ATV: Scenic Driving
- Biking: Mountain Biking; Road Biking
- Camping: Backcountry; Front-country (car); Horse/Stock Camping; RV Camping
- Fishing: Saltwater Fishing
- Food: Picnicking
- Tours: Guided; Self-Guided (Walking, Auto)
- Hiking: Backcountry; Front-country
- Equestrian: Horse Trekking; Horseback Riding; Horse Camping
- Paddling: Canoeing; Kayaking; Stand Up Paddleboarding; Whitewater Rafting
