 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 11:08:02,052 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here’s a concise, evidence-backed guide to Firefox on NixOS (and nix-darwin), covering what’s available, how dependencies/wrapping work, powerful Home Manager options, and how it compares to alternatives on macOS.

What’s available right now (channels and variants)
- NixOS channels discovered
  - stable = 25.11; also 25.05; unstable available (source: “NixOS Channels (auto-discovered)”)
- Firefox (source builds)
  - stable: firefox 145.0.2 (nixos_info)
  - unstable: firefox 142.0.1 (nixos_info)
  - ESR: appears via the firefox attribute as “140.5.0esr” on stable/25.05 searches (nixos_search), but a top-level package named firefox-esr was not found in stable/unstable (nixos_info). Practically: ESR is present in search results as a variant of firefox; the dedicated firefox-esr attribute may not be exposed in your current channels.
- Firefox binary
  - stable: firefox-bin 145.0.2 (nixos_info)
  - Version history and pinning metadata available (nixhub_package_versions firefox-bin)
- Unwrapped vs wrapped
  - firefox-unwrapped 142.0.1 exists on unstable (nixos_info). The wrapped firefox is what you typically install; unwrapped is the raw build used by the wrapper.
- Alternatives in nixpkgs (selected)
  - LibreWolf: 145.0.1-2 (nixos_info), versions available for pinning (nixhub_package_versions librewolf)
  - Floorp: 11.30.0 (nixos_info)
  - Chromium: 142.0.7444.175 (nixos_info); pin history (nixhub_package_versions chromium)
  - Brave: 1.84.141 (nixos_info; versions via nixhub)
  - Ungoogled Chromium: 139.0.7258.154 (nixos_search)
  - Tor Browser: 15.0.2 (nixhub_package_versions), package tor-browser shows in search (nixos_search)

How Firefox is packaged and how dependencies are managed
- Wrapped vs unwrapped
  - firefox is a wrapped package; firefox-unwrapped is the raw build. Home Manager explicitly says for stateVersion ≥ 19.09 you should use a wrapped Firefox package (home_manager_info programs.firefox.package).
  - NixOS exposes programs.firefox.wrapperConfig to pass arguments to the wrapper (nixos_info programs.firefox.wrapperConfig). The wrapper is what wires up runtime env vars, plugins/hosts, codecs, etc.
- Language packs and native messaging
  - NixOS: programs.firefox.languagePacks with a long validated list of codes (nixos_info programs.firefox.languagePacks).
  - Native messaging hosts can be added via programs.firefox.nativeMessagingHosts.packages and also toggled per well-known hosts (ff2mpv, gsconnect, passff, tridactyl, etc.) under programs.firefox.nativeMessagingHosts.* (nixos_search options result and nixos_info for specific toggles).
- Enterprise policies and managed preferences
  - NixOS: programs.firefox.policies (JSON) and programs.firefox.preferences (attrset). Using these will show “Your browser is managed by your organization”; NixOS clarifies why (nixos_info programs.firefox.preferences).
  - AutoConfig is supported via programs.firefox.autoConfig and programs.firefox.autoConfigFiles (nixos_info).
- GNOME integration
  - Home Manager can enable GNOME Shell native host connector (programs.firefox.enableGnomeExtensions) and instructs you to set services.gnome.gnome-browser-connector.enable = true (home_manager_info and nixos_info for that service).
- Progressive Web Apps
  - firefoxpwa package 2.17.0 (nixos_info firefoxpwa) and Home Manager module programs.firefoxpwa.* (home_manager_search) let you declaratively define PWA profiles and site entries.

Home Manager: powerful Firefox configuration you might not know about
- Top-level toggles and packaging
  - programs.firefox.enable
  - programs.firefox.package (use the wrapped package) and programs.firefox.finalPackage (home_manager_info)
  - programs.firefox.languagePacks, nativeMessagingHosts, pkcs11Modules, policies, enableGnomeExtensions, darwinDefaultsId (home_manager_info/search)
- Profiles (big feature)
  - programs.firefox.profiles.<name> with:
    - settings (about:config prefs), preConfig/extraConfig
    - bookmarks (declarative), containers (Multi-Account Containers with force replace), search (default/privateDefault/order/engines with force), extensions (packages + per-extension settings/permissions with exact/exhaustive permissions), userChrome/userContent CSS (home_manager_search/home_manager_options_by_prefix)
- PWAs for Firefox
  - programs.firefoxpwa.enable and rich per-profile/per-site options including creating desktop entries and icon assignment (home_manager_search)

NixOS module-level options for Firefox (system)
- programs.firefox.enable/package/wrapperConfig/preferences/policies/autoConfig/autoConfigFiles/languagePacks/nativeMessagingHosts.* toggles (nixos_search options result + nixos_info for each option)
- Service: services.firefox-syncserver.enable (self-hosted Firefox Sync storage) with clear caveats and DB setup notes; also a convenience singleNode option exists (nixos_info)

nix-darwin: how it stacks up and ways to manage Firefox on macOS
- No dedicated nix-darwin programs.firefox module (darwin_search found none).
- Common patterns:
  1) Use Home Manager on macOS to manage Firefox configuration declaratively (the HM firefox module works cross-platform; HM exposes programs.firefox.darwinDefaultsId for policy setup on macOS).
  2) Manage the app binary via Homebrew casks in nix-darwin:
     - homebrew.enable; homebrew.casks = [ "firefox" "librewolf" "brave-browser" "tor-browser" ... ] (darwin_options_by_prefix shows homebrew.casks and rich caskArgs; darwin_info homebrew.casks).
     - You can enable greedy upgrades per-cask or globally via homebrew.greedyCasks/caskArgs (darwin_options_by_prefix).
  3) Alternatively, include nixpkgs’ Firefox in environment.systemPackages (darwin_info environment.systemPackages), though in practice most macOS users prefer casks for app bundles.
- Summary: nix-darwin itself doesn’t configure Firefox, but pairing Home Manager (for policies/profiles/etc.) with Homebrew casks (for the app) gives you parity with NixOS’s declarative experience.

Pinning and choosing specific versions
- You can pin nixpkgs to a specific commit to obtain an exact Firefox/LibreWolf/Chromium version. nixhub provides commit hashes and timestamps for many versions:
  - firefox (nixhub_package_versions firefox)
  - firefox-bin (nixhub_package_versions firefox-bin)
  - firefox-esr history available (nixhub_package_versions firefox-esr), even though a dedicated firefox-esr attribute wasn’t found in current stable/unstable nixos_info queries.
  - librewolf (nixhub_package_versions librewolf)
  - chromium, brave, tor-browser similarly have version histories (nixhub_package_versions)
- Practical note on ESR: searches show ESR builds under the firefox attribute (e.g., “firefox (140.5.0esr)” in stable/25.05 search results). If your channel lacks a top-level firefox-esr attribute, you can still pin a nixpkgs revision where ESR is present as the firefox derivation’s version, or use a package set variant if exposed in that revision.

Quick-start examples

- NixOS (system configuration)
  - Basic:
    - programs.firefox.enable = true
    - programs.firefox.languagePacks = [ "en-US" "de" ]
    - programs.firefox.preferences = { "browser.tabs.tabmanager.enabled" = false; }
    - programs.firefox.policies = { ExtensionSettings = { ... }; }
  - GNOME integration:
    - programs.firefox.enableGnomeExtensions = true
    - services.gnome.gnome-browser-connector.enable = true

- Home Manager (user configuration; cross-platform)
  - programs.firefox.enable = true
  - programs.firefox.package = pkgs.firefox
  - programs.firefox.profiles.work = {
      isDefault = true;
      settings = { "privacy.trackingprotection.enabled" = true; };
      extensions = {
        packages = [ /* pre-packaged add-ons */ ];
        settings = { /* per-extension JSON options/permissions */ };
      };
      search = {
        default = "DuckDuckGo";
        privateDefault = "DuckDuckGo";
        force = true;
      };
      bookmarks = { force = true; settings = [ /* bookmarks tree */ ]; };
      containers = { containersForce = true; /* define containers */ };
    }
  - PWAs:
    - programs.firefoxpwa.enable = true
    - programs.firefoxpwa.profiles.<ULID>.sites.<ULID> = { name = "App"; url = "https://example.com"; desktopEntry.enable = true; }

- nix-darwin + Home Manager (macOS)
  - nix-darwin:
    - homebrew.enable = true
    - homebrew.casks = [ "firefox" "librewolf" "brave-browser" ]
  - Home Manager:
    - reuse the same programs.firefox.* configuration as on NixOS (plus darwinDefaultsId if you use policies)

Alternatives: when to pick what
- Firefox vs Firefox ESR
  - ESR favors stability/long-term support; mainline gets the newest features. On current channels, ESR appears as an alternate firefox version in search results.
- Firefox vs firefox-bin
  - firefox-bin is Mozilla’s binary; updates land quickly and sometimes differ slightly from source-built timings. It’s convenient if you want the upstream release cadence.
- LibreWolf/Floorp
  - LibreWolf: privacy-hardened Firefox derivative (nixos_info librewolf).
  - Floorp: featureful Firefox fork balancing privacy and UI customization (nixos_info floorp).
- Chromium/Brave/Ungoogled/Tor Browser
  - Chromium/Brave: Chromium ecosystem options with different privacy/feature tradeoffs (nixos_info chromium, brave; nixos_search ungoogled-chromium).
  - Tor Browser: for Tor-network anonymity (nixos_search tor-browser; versions via nixhub).

Where to start
- If you want a declarative, repeatable Firefox setup:
  - On NixOS: use programs.firefox + Home Manager profiles to declaratively manage prefs, extensions, search, containers, and policies.
  - On macOS: install the app via homebrew.casks in nix-darwin, and use Home Manager’s Firefox module to configure everything identically to Linux.
- If you need a specific version:
  - Use nixhub to grab a commit hash, pin nixpkgs to it, and install firefox/firefox-bin/librewolf at that revision.

