2) Shortlist: SF corridors that balance views, foot traffic, and delivery access
Use the weather numbers above to time pop-ups or peak hours (e.g., afternoons on 12/01 and 12/03 are the mildest). Pair that with corridors that traditionally have strong pedestrian activity and good logistics.

Top candidates to evaluate on the ground (use the data sources in Section 3 to back these up with hard counts):
- Embarcadero (Ferry Building to Pier 7)
  - Why: Unmatched bay/bridge views; high pedestrian volumes; direct transit access (BART/Muni at Embarcadero).
  - Weather implication: Waterfront is more wind-exposed; this week’s citywide and Marina wind maxima (12–16 kph) suggest simple wind screens and heat lamps will make it comfortable during midafternoon on 12/01 and 12/03.
  - Validate with data: SFMTA automated pedestrian counters near Market St; Port and Ferry Building event calendars; SFMTA curb/“color curb” maps for loading zones.

- Marina District — Chestnut St (Fillmore to Divisadero)
  - Why: Established dining corridor near Marina Green/Crissy Field views; steady local foot traffic; relatively moderate winds vs. direct waterfront.
  - Weather evidence: See Marina District forecast above (sunny 12/01, foggy 12/02, mild 12/03; max wind ≤15.5 kph; humidity drops to ~67% on 12/03).
  - Validate: Shared Spaces/parklet density map, business registrations (restaurant count), SFMTA loading zones on Chestnut/Fillmore.

- North Beach — Columbus Ave near Washington Square
  - Why: Tourist and local draw; iconic vistas (Saints Peter and Paul, Coit Tower sightlines); many adjacent restaurants to create a “district effect.”
  - Weather proxy: Use SF citywide + nearby Marina/Richmond numbers; expect similar temps/winds; plan for wind mitigation on corners.
  - Validate: Automated pedestrian counts near Columbus/Broadway, Shared Spaces map for existing parklets (to show precedent), curb loading map.

- Hayes Valley — Hayes St around Patricia’s Green / Octavia
  - Why: Strong all-day foot traffic, central location, less wind exposure than waterfront; popular outdoor setups since Shared Spaces.
  - Weather proxy: Use SF citywide; slightly warmer feel vs. ocean-facing neighborhoods. Good on 12/01 and 12/03 afternoons.
  - Validate: Parklet count and clustering, SFMTA counts around Market/Octavia, curb availability on Hayes/Octavia.

- Mission — Valencia St (16th to 20th)
  - Why: Dense restaurant cluster; high evening foot traffic; center-running bikeway pilot improved operations for curbside uses nearby.
  - Weather proxy: Use citywide numbers; leeward/east side generally avoids heavy fog seen in Sunset on 12/02 (use citywide vs. Sunset contrast).
  - Validate: Automated counters near 16th/Valencia, Shared Spaces participation, loading zones on cross streets (e.g., 18th/19th).

3) How to back your site picks with hard numbers (verifiable sources)
Use the steps below to turn the shortlist into a defensible, numbers-forward proposal.

- Weather (near-term comfort windows)
  - Source: WeatherAPI (used above via tool). Retain the exact forecasts and “last_updated” timestamps in your appendix.
  - Action: Cite the specific daily max/min temp, humidity, wind, and rain chance for your target corridor’s week-of opening. For neighborhoods that don’t resolve cleanly, cite SF citywide + the nearest correct neighborhood (e.g., Marina for North Beach/Embarcadero).

- Pedestrian volumes (foot traffic)
  - Source: SFMTA Automated Pedestrian Counters (APCS) on SF Open Data.
    - Portal: https://data.sfgov.org (search: “Automated Pedestrian Counters” or “pedestrian counters SFMTA”)
  - Action: Pull average hourly/daily counts nearest your block (e.g., Market/Embarcadero; Powell/Market; Columbus/Broadway; Octavia/Market; 16th/Valencia). Quote recent-year averages and peak-hour ranges.

- Existing outdoor dining density (social proof + competitive cluster)
  - Source: Shared Spaces locations (SF’s permanent program)
    - Program: https://sf.gov/program/shared-spaces
    - Map/data: Linked from the program page and SF Open Data/ArcGIS (search “Shared Spaces locations”)
  - Action: Count existing permitted parklets within 250 m of your proposed address. Show “X parklets within 250 m” as evidence of corridor viability.

- Restaurant concentration (supplier and partner adjacency)
  - Source: Registered Business Locations (SF Open Data; filter NAICS 7225/722511 etc. for restaurants)
    - Portal: https://data.sfgov.org (search: “Registered Business Locations”)
  - Action: Count restaurants within 250 m and 500 m buffers for each candidate block; report totals and cuisine mix if useful.

- Delivery and curb access (operations)
  - Source: SFMTA Curb Management / Color Curb Map (commercial loading zones, tow-away, etc.)
    - Overview: https://www.sfmta.com/maps (search: “Curb management” / “Color Curb”)
  - Action: Map yellow (commercial loading) zones and any time-of-day restrictions within 100–150 m. Recommend blocks with at least 1–2 loading zones on the same blockface or cross street during your delivery windows.

- Transit access (demand + staff access)
  - Sources:
    - Muni stops/lines (SFMTA GTFS or route maps): https://www.sfmta.com/maps
    - BART station entries/exists (BART ridership reports): https://www.bart.gov/about/reports/ridership
  - Action: Report the nearest frequent Muni lines and the distance to the nearest BART/Muni Rail stations; include average weekday entries where applicable.

4) What the numbers above suggest (initial, defensible takeaways)
- Timing: This week offers dry days with moderate wind; 12/01 and 12/03 are the best for comfort citywide (max temps ~13–16.5°C; wind ≤16 kph; humidity down to ~67% on 12/03 in Marina/Richmond).
- Westside vs. bayside: Sunset shows cooler temps and dense fog on 12/02 (max 11.4°C, avg humidity 93%). If you need reliable daytime warmth for seating, favor bayside/leeward corridors (Marina, North Beach, Embarcadero, Hayes Valley, Mission) over the ocean-facing westside.
- Waterfront views vs. wind: Embarcadero and Marina Green/Crissy Field corridors deliver the best views, but plan wind screens (winds ~12–16 kph in the forecast window). Chestnut St (slightly inland) can be a good compromise.
- Delivery logistics: Begin with corridors with proven Shared Spaces/parklet presence and yellow-zone availability (e.g., Chestnut, Hayes, Valencia). Use SFMTA curb maps to confirm loading windows match your delivery schedule.

5) Quick, evidence-based shortlist to take forward
- Embarcadero (Ferry Building–Pier 7): Views; highest regional connectivity. Use APCS counts near Market/Embarcadero and BART station data for your appendix.
- Marina District — Chestnut St (Fillmore–Divisadero): Good microclimate this week (see Marina forecast); strong corridor identity; check yellow zones on Chestnut/Fillmore.
- North Beach — Columbus Ave near Washington Square: Strong destination appeal; verify pedestrian counts and parklet presence via Shared Spaces.
- Hayes Valley — Hayes St at Patricia’s Green: Wind-sheltered relative to waterfront; strong Shared Spaces precedent.
- Mission — Valencia St (16th–20th): Dense cluster of eateries; evening foot traffic; verify curb/loading on cross streets.

Caveats on weather data resolution
- Neighborhood queries that mis-resolved to non-California places (e.g., “Mission District, San Francisco” → Mexico; “Embarcadero” → Mexico; “North Beach” → Mexico; “Inner Sunset” → Switzerland; “Ocean Beach” → New Zealand) were not used. I relied on SF citywide plus correctly resolved neighborhoods (Marina District, Richmond District, Sunset District) and nearby-city proxies (Oakland, Redwood City, Palo Alto, Half Moon Bay, Tiburon, Alameda) that the tool resolved correctly in California.

